 "id": "safety/patch-copy-doubling",
 "title": "Forty copies of a document into itself must be refused with a limit error, not grown to terabytes",
 "category": "safety",
 "origin": "bug",
 "productions": [
  "187"
 ],
 "refs": [
  "RFC 6902 section 4.5",
  "docs/edit-semantics.md"
 ],
 "notes": "Each copy of the whole document into a new member doubles it, so forty of them would make an output of more than twenty terabytes (over a hundred in block style) from a twenty-byte input and a patch of two kilobytes. Every copy is valid on its own (RFC 6902 allows a copy of a value into a location inside it), so the only acceptable outcome is a limit error: the family rule is that the bytes a patch copies, aliases written out included, are charged to the same byte budget as alias expansion, and a patch that goes over it fails with a limit error and changes nothing. The failed patch leaves the document as it was (RFC 6902 section 5), and the case states it: a library that reports a document after the refusal must report the input bytes, as well as staying inside the bounds. A library that does the copies without a bound fails on memory or time.",
 "provenance": {
  "reference": "A patch engine that copied a whole document into itself with no bound, so repeated copies grew the output exponentially."
 },
